1
00:00:02,400 --> 00:00:05,868
People have told hero stories for as long as they have told stories.

2
00:00:06,228 --> 00:00:08,549
One of the oldest, the Epic of Gilgamesh,

3
00:00:08,549 --> 00:00:11,776
was written in Mesopotamia about four thousand years ago.

4
00:00:12,136 --> 00:00:13,617
The Greeks had Achilles,

5
00:00:13,617 --> 00:00:15,591
medieval Europe had its knights,

6
00:00:15,591 --> 00:00:17,318
and we have films and games.

7
00:00:17,678 --> 00:00:21,365
The Swiss psychiatrist Carl Jung called the hero an archetype:

8
00:00:21,365 --> 00:00:23,328
a pattern that keeps reappearing.

9
00:00:23,688 --> 00:00:29,417
In 1949, the American mythologist Joseph Campbell published The Hero with a Thousand Faces.

10
00:00:29,777 --> 00:00:31,408
By faces, he meant people:

11
00:00:31,408 --> 00:00:34,482
each hero is another instance of the same figure.

12
00:00:34,842 --> 00:00:41,367
The Legend of Zelda games show this in miniature: in each era, Link is a new person, and still the hero.

13
00:00:41,727 --> 00:00:45,077
Every hero story is a kind of map for facing the unknown.

14
00:00:45,437 --> 00:00:52,162
Campbell drew a map of those maps: the hero leaves home, is tested, and returns changed.

15
00:00:53,012 --> 00:00:58,349
Across cultures, heroes share qualities: courage, persistence, care for others,

16
00:00:58,349 --> 00:01:01,322
and a willingness to face what others avoid.

17
00:01:01,682 --> 00:01:05,064
But why would cultures that never met keep inventing the same figure?

18
00:01:05,914 --> 00:01:10,356
Biology has a name for this kind of pattern: convergent evolution.

19
00:01:10,716 --> 00:01:15,252
Distantly related species that face the same problem often reach similar solutions.

20
00:01:15,612 --> 00:01:18,550
Insects, birds and bats each evolved wings,

21
00:01:18,550 --> 00:01:22,309
and octopuses and humans each evolved camera-like eyes.

22
00:01:22,669 --> 00:01:29,049
Cultures converge too: writing was invented separately in Mesopotamia, China and Mesoamerica.

23
00:01:29,409 --> 00:01:32,941
Hero stories may be another example of cultural convergence.

24
00:01:33,301 --> 00:01:37,198
Every culture faces problems and benefits from people who can solve them,

25
00:01:37,198 --> 00:01:39,867
so stories about those people keep being invented.

26
00:01:40,717 --> 00:01:45,447
That is convergence in stories—but there is a second convergence, in real people.

27
00:01:45,807 --> 00:01:47,336
Across times and cultures,

28
00:01:47,336 --> 00:01:51,689
real individuals separately arrive at the same capacity to solve problems,

29
00:01:51,689 --> 00:01:56,100
even though most societies throughout history have reliably held them back.

30
00:01:56,460 --> 00:02:01,172
The philosopher Karl Popper argued that knowledge grows by finding and correcting errors.

31
00:02:01,532 --> 00:02:03,736
A hero does this reliably, in themselves,

32
00:02:03,736 --> 00:02:09,273
and better than the culture around them—which makes them a revolutionary force at the level of culture.

33
00:02:09,633 --> 00:02:13,763
In Hayao Miyazaki's 1984 film Nausicaä of the Valley of the Wind,

34
00:02:13,763 --> 00:02:16,304
everyone fears a spreading toxic jungle.

35
00:02:16,664 --> 00:02:19,076
Nausicaä refuses the dogma, studies it,

36
00:02:19,076 --> 00:02:22,044
and finds that it is cleaning the poisoned soil.

37
00:02:22,404 --> 00:02:24,116
Her judgement isn't perfect,

38
00:02:24,116 --> 00:02:27,783
but it is reliable enough to face problems no one predicted,

39
00:02:27,783 --> 00:02:29,984
in familiar places and unknown ones.

40
00:02:30,834 --> 00:02:32,507
In The Beginning of Infinity,

41
00:02:32,507 --> 00:02:36,027
the physicist David Deutsch argues that problems are soluble,

42
00:02:36,027 --> 00:02:37,527
given the right knowledge.

43
00:02:37,887 --> 00:02:40,709
A hero treats all problems alike: a monster,

44
00:02:40,709 --> 00:02:44,045
a famine and a disease are all things to understand.

45
00:02:44,405 --> 00:02:50,768
That is the hero's reach—and why a single error-correcting mind can overturn an entire culture's limits.

46
00:02:51,618 --> 00:02:54,235
Because stories are maps of these people,

47
00:02:54,235 --> 00:02:57,491
the hero is often rediscovered, and often improved.

48
00:02:57,851 --> 00:02:59,660
Gilgamesh was a great warrior,

49
00:02:59,660 --> 00:03:02,795
but his epic opens with him oppressing his own city.

50
00:03:03,155 --> 00:03:06,522
In Homer's Iliad, Achilles is the greatest fighter at Troy,

51
00:03:06,522 --> 00:03:09,831
yet his pride keeps him from battle while his friends die.

52
00:03:10,191 --> 00:03:15,958
Many past heroes were heroic in one domain and bound by their culture's blind spots in others.

53
00:03:16,318 --> 00:03:19,534
Later stories keep what worked and correct what did not.

54
00:03:20,384 --> 00:03:22,459
Campbell counted a thousand faces.

55
00:03:22,819 --> 00:03:29,578
For most of history, this kind of person was rare—reliably held back by static cultures that feared disruption.

56
00:03:29,938 --> 00:03:35,400
But if a hero is anyone who corrects their own errors and treats every problem as soluble,

57
00:03:35,400 --> 00:03:36,856
that force has no limit.
